Consider a quantum network modeled as a graph G=(V,E) whose vertices represent quantum nodes (end nodes and quantum repeaters capable of entanglement swapping) and whose edges represent physical links along which short-range bipartite entangled pairs can be generated. A multi-hop end-to-end entangled state between a source s∈V and a destination d∈V is produced by generating link-level entangled pairs along a path P=(s=v0,v1,…,vk=d) and then performing entanglement-swapping operations at the intermediate nodes.

For an edge e∈E and a path P, associate the following per-edge and per-path quantities:
(i) the entanglement-swapping time τ(e) and the cumulative swapping time along P;
(ii) the end-to-end fidelity F(P)=∏ f(e) (with f(e)∈(0,1] the per-link fidelity, modified by swapping);
(iii) the achievable throughput T(P) (expected number of end-to-end entangled pairs successfully delivered per time slot), which depends on per-link generation probabilities and on swap success probabilities in a non-multiplicative way once decoherence and memory cut-offs are included;
(iv) the consumption C(P) of entangled resources required to realize one end-to-end pair;
(v) the delay D(P), including waiting times caused by synchronization of link-level entanglements at swapping nodes;
(vi) a resource-competition penalty R(P;{Pj}) reflecting that other concurrent source-destination requests use overlapping edges and memories.

Define an aggregate route cost c(P) as a function of (τ,F,T,C,D,R). The metric c is in general non-additive: c(P1∘P2)≠c(P1)+c(P2). Equivalently, the achievable throughput along a concatenation of two sub-paths is not the sum (or any simple combination) of the throughputs of the sub-paths.

Problem. Develop a principled route-selection framework that, given (G, source-destination request(s), per-edge stochastic models for entanglement generation and swapping, memory decoherence times, and a set of concurrent requests), outputs a path (or a set of paths) optimizing—or Pareto-optimizing—the vector of objectives (T(P), F(P), C(P), D(P)) subject to a resource-competition model R, while correctly handling waiting-time computations at swapping nodes. In particular:

(a) Identify conditions on the cost function c (e.g., monotonicity, isotonicity) under which a generalized Dijkstra-type algorithm provably returns an optimal path despite non-additivity, and exhibit such an algorithm together with its complexity.

(b) When c does not satisfy such conditions, design an approximation or heuristic routing protocol with provable performance guarantees on at least one of throughput, fidelity, resource consumption, or delay.

(c) Provide a method for computing or bounding the expected waiting times at intermediate repeaters under realistic memory decoherence, and incorporate the resulting estimates into the cost.

(d) Extend the framework to concurrent multi-pair requests, giving a scheduling/routing scheme that manages resource competition.

